    <h2>Who I Am</h2>
    <img src="/assets/images/avatar.png" alt="Illustrated portrait of Fabrizio Palladino" class="about__portrait">
    <p style="margin-top: var(--fp-space-sm);">
      I'm a senior frontend developer with ten years of freelance work in Brussels, most of it for EU institutions.
      Since 2020 I've worked at the Council of the European Union, where my role covers the frontend end to end: advising on UX, maintaining and refactoring the frontend architecture of the Council's website, and building the Council's design system from scratch, a library of web components distributed via npm and used by the Council's website and two third-party projects.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--fp-space-sm);">
      I came to frontend from the humanities. My degree combined digital communication, publishing management and behavioural science, and I learned frontend hands-on, by building.
      That mix lets me explain ideas clearly and lead conversations with stakeholders, and it shapes how I build: semantic, well-structured markup and interfaces that fit the people who use them.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--fp-space-sm);">
      The work I care about most lives below the framework layer — in semantics, in accessibility, in the parts of an interface most users never name but always feel.
    </p>
    <blockquote style="margin-top: var(--fp-space-md);">
      Frameworks change every year, people don't.
    </blockquote>
  </div>
</section>

